# CloudflarePurge

https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Extension:CloudflarePurge

Fork of the upstream WMF extension, with:

- purging of every page affected by an edit, not just the edited page;
- support for purging additional hostnames (e.g. kiosk subdomains)
  alongside the canonical URL.

## What gets purged

Upstream hooks `PageSaveComplete` / `PageDeleteComplete` and purges the
one URL of the page that was saved or deleted. That leaves every page
which *transcludes* the edited page stale at the edge for a full TTL —
which for a wiki whose templates carry benefit amounts and eligibility
thresholds is a correctness problem, not a latency one.

MediaWiki already knows the full set. `WikiPage::onArticleEdit()` queues
`HTMLCacheUpdateJob` for the `templatelinks`, `imagelinks` and `redirect`
backlinks of the edited title; those jobs partition the backlinks into
ranges of `$wgUpdateRowsPerJob` and collate each range into leaf jobs of
`$wgUpdateRowsPerQuery` titles, then hand the resulting URLs to
`CdnCacheUpdate::purge()`. That method broadcasts them on the
`cdn-url-purges` event channel *before* falling back to `$wgCdnServers`.

So this extension subscribes to that channel (`CloudflarePurgeRelayer`)
rather than walking backlinks itself. The fan-out, the batching, the
de-duplication and the job-queue scheduling are core's. When the relay is
active the two page hooks stand down, because core already purges the
edited page itself.

`$wgCdnServers` may stay empty — the relayer is invoked regardless of it.

## Configuration

| Variable | Type | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `$wgCloudflarePurgeZoneID` | string | `""` | Cloudflare zone ID |
| `$wgCloudflarePurgeToken` | string | `""` | API token with Zone.Cache Purge permission (recommended) |
| `$wgCloudflarePurgeAuthEmail` | string | `""` | Legacy auth: account email |
| `$wgCloudflarePurgeAuthKey` | string | `""` | Legacy auth: global API key |
| `$wgCloudflarePurgeExtraHosts` | array | `[]` | Additional hostnames to purge (same path/query, different host) |
| `$wgCloudflarePurgeUseCdnRelay` | bool | `true` | Subscribe to `cdn-url-purges`. Set to `false` to fall back to purging only the edited page. |
| `$wgCloudflarePurgeMaxUrlsPerBatch` | int | `1000` | Ceiling on URLs per purge call; `0` disables the ceiling |
| `$wgCloudflarePurgeUrlsPerRequest` | int | `100` | URLs per Cloudflare API request |
| `$wgCloudflarePurgeMaxRetries` | int | `2` | Retries for a *retryable* failure: HTTP 429, 5xx, or a transport error a retry could fix |
| `$wgCloudflarePurgePreSendBudgetSeconds` | float | `5` | Wall-clock seconds one purge call may spend *before the response is flushed*. `0` removes the bound. Never applies to CLI or post-send job purges. |

## Extra hosts

When `$wgCloudflarePurgeExtraHosts` is set, every purged URL is also
purged under each extra host, keeping the same path and query.

```php
$wgCloudflarePurgeExtraHosts = [ 'kiosk.kolzchut.org.il' ];
```

Extra hosts multiply the fan-out, so they are counted against
`$wgCloudflarePurgeMaxUrlsPerBatch` rather than added on top of it.

## Fan-out and rate limits

Cloudflare's limits are **per account**, not per zone, and they apply
across every zone on the same plan tier. Figures below are from
Cloudflare's "Purge cache" documentation, which publishes them as *two*
separate tables (verified 2026-08-23):

- **Operations per request:** 100 for single-file purge on
  Free/Pro/Business, 500 on Enterprise. The 500 is specific to
  single-file purge — purge by hostname, tag or prefix is capped at 100
  per request on *every* plan, Enterprise included.
- **Requests:** 5/minute on Free, 5/second on Pro, 10/second on Business,
  50/second on Enterprise. This row is in the *hostname, tag, prefix and
  purge-everything* table.
- **URLs:** 800/s on Free, 1500/s on Pro and Business, 3000/s on
  Enterprise. This row is in the *single-file (by URL)* table.

Requests are split at `$wgCloudflarePurgeUrlsPerRequest`. Which limit binds
for *this* extension turns on reading those two tables as **disjoint**,
which is a reading rather than a stated fact — Cloudflare's own heading for
the first is "Hostname, tag, prefix URL, and purge everything limits", and
that stray "URL" is ambiguous.

The reading taken here is that the request-rate row does *not* meter
single-file purge, i.e. that `{"files": [...]}` — all this extension ever
sends — is governed by the URLs/second row alone. Two things support it:
the sibling table is titled "Single-file purge limits", and the max-ops
rows differ (100 vs 500 on Enterprise), which only makes sense if the two
tables cover disjoint operations.

**If that reading is wrong**, a Free-plan account is capped at 5 purge
*requests* per minute, which would bind long before 800 URLs/s and would
make `$wgCloudflarePurgeUrlsPerRequest` the setting that matters most.
Worth re-checking against Cloudflare's page before relying on the fan-out
figures below on a Free plan; on Pro and above (5+/second) the distinction
is academic at this extension's volumes.

What bounds a single call is core, not this extension. Backlink purges
arrive one leaf `HTMLCacheUpdateJob` at a time, and a leaf job carries at
most `$wgUpdateRowsPerQuery` (default **100**) pages — `$wgUpdateRowsPerJob`
(300) is the size of the backlink *range* a partition job covers, not the
number of titles a leaf job is given (`BacklinkJobUtils::partitionBacklinkJob()`
takes the two separately). One URL per page — backlink purges pass
`PURGE_URLS_LINKSUPDATE_ONLY`, so no `action=history` or `action=raw`
variants are added — multiplied by one plus the number of extra hosts.
With one extra host the worst case is **200 URLs, in 2 requests**, per
call. `$wgCloudflarePurgeMaxUrlsPerBatch` (1000) therefore does not bind in
normal operation; it is a circuit breaker for a caller that hands over an
unbounded list, and tripping it is logged at error level because the
discarded pages will stay stale.

A widely transcluded template still produces a large *total* fan-out. The
worst case on the Hebrew main wiki is a template transcluded by 8,289
pages, which with one extra host is **16,578 URLs in 166 requests** — but
it arrives as a stream of jobs, so the job queue, not this extension, is
what paces it.

## Failure behaviour

A failed purge is logged to the `CloudflarePurge` channel and reported
through the return value; it is never thrown.

Throwing is the worse failure in both of the places this code runs from.
From a job, a throw loses the purge outright on an installation with no
working retry path — while the `page_touched` bump the job already made
makes a re-run a no-op anyway. From `PageSaveComplete` it is worse still:
that hook runs inside the `AtomicSectionUpdate` that `PageUpdater` wraps
around `DerivedPageDataUpdater::doUpdates()`, so an exception does not
merely fail the purge — it cancels that atomic section and rolls back the
links and site-stats work that came with the edit. (The edit itself still
reports success: the update is deferred, and `DeferredUpdates::run()`
catches the exception.)

**The log line is the only signal.** `CdnCacheUpdate::purge()` discards the
return value of the event relayer, so nothing upstream can see a failure.
A wiki running this extension must route the `CloudflarePurge` channel
somewhere real — under a `MonologSpi` with a `'@default' => null` fallback,
an unlisted channel is silently discarded and a failed purge becomes
invisible.

Retries are deliberately narrow, and they sort transport failures into
three classes rather than two. HTTP 429 and 5xx are retried
(`$wgCloudflarePurgeMaxRetries`) with a short exponential backoff, and a
`Retry-After` on a 429 is honoured up to 5 seconds — beyond that the
attempt is abandoned rather than slept through.

- **Permanent** (cURL 1, 3, 51, 58, 59, 60, 77, 83) — a malformed URL, a
  certificate that does not verify. One attempt, no retry: these fail
  identically every time.
- **Fast-retry** (cURL 5, 6, 35) — the full ladder normally, but capped at
  *one* retry when the call is running under the pre-send time budget.
  libcurl reports every resolver failure as errno 6, not only a name that
  does not exist: a SERVFAIL, a resolver timeout, a cycling Docker embedded
  DNS. Errno 35 is likewise libcurl's catch-all for a handshake that went
  wrong, which covers a mid-handshake reset. Treating these as permanent
  drops the purge on the first attempt during a sub-second blip — and
  nothing retries a dropped purge (see "The log line is the only signal"
  above). The cap reaches exactly as far as its justification does: where
  an editor is waiting, one retry recovers the blip and a full ladder would
  spend the whole budget re-asking a resolver that is genuinely down; on
  the job path there is no budget, nobody is waiting, and that path carries
  the backlink fan-out, so capping it would drop the larger URL set to save
  nothing.
- **Transient** (everything else, e.g. 7 and 28) — the full ladder.

Which class ended a failed purge is logged as `transport`.

Requests carry a 5-second connect timeout and a 15-second total timeout.
Without them cURL waits 300 seconds to connect and forever to finish.

### The pre-send time budget

Per-request timeouts bound one *attempt*. They do not bound the ladder,
and the ladder is what an editor waits for.

`WikiPage::onArticleEdit()` (`WikiPage.php:2565`) purges the edited page at
`:2588` with `PURGE_INTENT_TXROUND_REFLECTED`, which is
`PURGE_PRESEND|PURGE_REBOUND`; `WikiPage::doPurge()` (`:1187`) uses a bare
`PURGE_PRESEND` at `:1196`. Either way the CDN purge is deferred at
`DeferredUpdates::PRESEND` by `HTMLCacheUpdater::purgeUrls()`
(`HTMLCacheUpdater.php:148-149`) — before the response is flushed. (The
similar-looking pair at `:186-187` is the `$this->useFileCache` branch
queueing an `HtmlFileCacheUpdate`, which is not this.) Nothing in a normal
stack ends a purge that overruns there:

- PHP's `max_execution_time` is a CPU-time timer (`ITIMER_PROF`) and does
  not tick while the process is blocked in cURL or `usleep`. Measured: a
  script under `max_execution_time=2` ran 8 seconds of blocking cURL and
  `usleep` and was never interrupted.
- `$wgRequestTimeLimit` becomes a *wall-clock* limit only when the Excimer
  extension is installed; without it `RequestTimeout` falls back to
  `set_time_limit()`, i.e. the same CPU-time timer. It also defaults to
  `null`, in which case the limit is never armed at all.
- php-fpm's `request_terminate_timeout` is frequently left unset on wikis
  with legitimately slow endpoints.

So the request does not die — it **stalls**, holding a worker, until the
web server's own read timeout hands the editor a gateway error for a save
that in fact succeeded. During a Cloudflare outage every concurrent save
pays that at once, and the pool saturates.

Measured on the real clock against an endpoint that accepts the connection
and then stops answering, with the budget removed: **45.76 seconds for one
chunk** (3 attempts × 15s + 0.75s of backoff). A blackholed endpoint that
drops SYNs costs the connect timeout instead: 15.75s per chunk. Multiply
by the number of chunks.

`$wgCloudflarePurgePreSendBudgetSeconds` (default 5) is a wall-clock
deadline for the whole call. It clamps each attempt's cURL timeouts to
what is left, refuses a backoff that would not leave time for the attempt
it precedes, and stops between chunks — so the deadline is a real bound
rather than one checked only between attempts.

It applies **only** on the pre-send path. Two conditions separate that
from everything else, and both are needed: not being in CLI (which rules
out the dedicated job runner), and `headers_sent()` being false (which
rules out post-send work inside a web request — `$wgJobRunRate` defaults
to `1`, so a web request may run a job after its response is flushed).
Core makes the same distinction the same way. The job-queue path carries
the backlink fan-out and nobody is waiting on it; clipping it would trade
a bounded editor stall for exactly the stale pages this extension exists
to prevent.

Standing down is itself logged, because on its own it is indistinguishable
from the case it is not meant to cover. If anything writes output before
the pre-send deferred update runs — a stray debug print, an extension
echoing, an output buffer flushed early — `headers_sent()` is true on a
path where an editor genuinely *is* waiting, and the bound silently does
not arm. The only symptom would be a save that stalls for the full
un-budgeted ladder above, which is the failure the budget exists to
prevent and the hardest one to attribute afterwards.

So a *configured* budget that is declined writes one line, and the line
names which condition declined it:

```
Cloudflare pre-send purge budget of 5s not applied: cli           (debug)
Cloudflare pre-send purge budget of 5s not applied: headers-sent  (info)
```

Same message either way, so one grep finds both; the `reason` context key
and the level tell them apart. The levels follow how often each can
legitimately fire. `cli` is every purge the job runner makes, so it is
debug — the answer stays next to the purge it belongs to without a
per-job line reading as a finding. `headers-sent` is reached only by a
*web* request, the CLI test having already returned; on a wiki with
`$wgJobRunRate = 0` — the only configuration in which this budget is the
sole thing between an editor and a Cloudflare outage — no web request
runs a job after its response is flushed, so it cannot fire in normal
operation, and info makes it a rare line worth reading. A wiki that does
run jobs post-send sees it at that rate instead, where the reason code
says plainly that it is expected traffic.

A budget of `0` is **not** logged. Nothing was configured, so there is no
decline to report, and a line there would fire on every purge call of
every wiki that deliberately removed the bound — the kind of message
readers learn to filter, which would take the rare one with it.

When the budget does stop a call, the URLs it did not reach are handed to
MediaWiki's own `CdnPurgeJob` — the job core uses for its rebound purges —
so the clip is a *deferral*, not a loss. `CdnPurgeJob::run()` calls
`CdnCacheUpdate::purge()`, which broadcasts on `cdn-url-purges` and so
arrives back here, on the job runner, in CLI, with no budget and the full
ladder. Because the budget is never armed on that path, a deferred purge
cannot re-enter the branch that deferred it.

The budget can stop a call in **two** places, and both defer. It can refuse
to start a chunk, which is what happens on a multi-chunk call; and it can
cut off the ladder part-way through a chunk that has already begun, which
is what happens on the pre-send path, where — see below — the whole call is
a single chunk. Covering only the first would mean the deferral never fired
on the path it was written for: every clipped pre-send purge would be a
silent full-TTL stale page, and the budget would have introduced the exact
failure it was added to prevent. `sendChunk()` therefore reports *why* it
stopped, not just that it did.

The stop is logged at error level with the URL count, the reason, the
deadline that produced it, and whether the hand-over was accepted — because
a failed enqueue *is* a full-TTL stale page. One caveat the log wording
carries deliberately: `lazyPush()` registers the enqueue as a post-send
deferred update, so "queued **for** the job queue" means the hand-over was
accepted, not that the job reached Redis. A failure after that point is
reported by `DeferredUpdates`, not on this channel. Using `push()` would
make the claim exact at the cost of a Redis round-trip on the pre-send
path, which is the thing being bounded.

How many URLs a pre-send call actually carries: `HTMLCacheUpdater::getUrls()`
yields the page URL plus `action=history` when `PURGE_URLS_LINKSUPDATE_ONLY`
is absent (three for a JS/JSON/CSS config page), and just the page URL when
it is present, as on a backlink job purge. So a pre-send purge is one URL
or two, times one plus the number of extra hosts — a single chunk. The
budget therefore bounds the *ladder*, not a fan-out.

## Tests

`tests/phpunit/unit` holds dependency-free tests of the fan-out
arithmetic, the event-payload handling, the retry policy, the time budget
and the channel registration.

Two of them are about timing rather than arithmetic.
`CloudflarePurgeLadderTest` drives the real retry ladder on a virtual
clock, because reaching the un-budgeted 45.75s worst case in real time is
what stopped anyone noticing it. `CloudflarePurgeDeadlineWallClockTest`
then drives the real request path — real cURL, real timeouts, real clock —
at a local socket that accepts and never answers, on two different
budgets, to show that the clamped timeout reaches libcurl and is what ends
the attempt. It takes a couple of seconds; with the clamp removed the same
test takes 45.

`CloudflarePurgeBudgetGateTest` is about a third thing: whether the budget
armed, and whether it said so when it did not. Its four fast cases stub the
two conditions (`MW_ENTRY_POINT` is a constant, and this suite forbids
output, so neither is settable in-process) — but a stubbed seam cannot show
that the seam is wired to the function it names, so a fifth case runs the
gate in a subprocess that flushes real output and asks the real
`headers_sent()`. Verified by mutation: hard-coding the seam to `false`
leaves all four stubbed cases green and reddens only that one.

`tests/phpunit/integration` is an upgrade canary rather than a test of
this extension: it asserts that *MediaWiki* still queues backlink jobs and
still broadcasts their URLs on `cdn-url-purges`, which is the assumption
the whole extension rests on. It deliberately stops at the channel, with a
capturing relayer standing in for `CloudflarePurgeRelayer` — going further
would mean issuing a real purge against a real Cloudflare zone. It needs a
database.

### Running them

The unit suite needs no MediaWiki, no `LocalSettings.php` and no database
— only PHP with ext-curl and PHPUnit, with an autoloader built from
`extension.json`. So it is a plain PHPUnit run, not `tests/phpunit/phpunit.php`:

```sh
composer update          # composer.lock is not committed
composer phpunit         # 60 tests, ~2.2s
composer test            # parallel-lint + phpcs + minus-x
```

`.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs both on every push to `kolzchut-main` and
on every pull request.

`phpunit.xml.dist` sets `failOnSkipped`, and that is deliberate. The
wall-clock test skips itself when it cannot open a loopback socket or when
ext-curl is missing — which would leave a sandbox printing a green
"OK, but ... skipped" while the suite had silently lost its only
real-request coverage. Verified: forcing the listen to fail leaves a
default run green at exit 0 with 2 skipped, and red with the setting on.

`tests/phpunit/integration` is not part of that run — it is excluded from
`phpunit.xml.dist` because it needs a real wiki and a database, and runs
under MediaWiki's own runner.
